  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nUpper level trough axis is now moving east of the region across\nNew England.  At the surface, cold front continues to slowly \npush east across the western Atlantic Ocean, while high pressure\nis located well west of the region over the Midwest. Visible\nsatellite imagery continues to show bands of high clouds across\nfar southeastern areas that are lifting northeast parallel to\nthe departing surface boundary. Meanwhile, some diurnal cumulus\nis ongoing, mainly across the high terrain. Through this\nevening, the high clouds across southeastern areas will be\nslowly departing off to the east, although it may take some time\nbased on satellite trends. The diurnal cumulus should diminish\nwith the loss of daytime heating. Overall, skies will be\ntrending to clear/mostly clear for the overnight hours.\n\nBreezy west to northwest winds have been ongoing across the area\nthanks to the cold advection and good mixing due to the cool\ntemps aloft. These winds will decrease tonight, although they\nmay keep up slightly this evening. Eventually, with cold\nadvection ending and high pressure building towards the area,\nwinds will become light to calm for the overnight hours. With\nthe clearing skies and light/calm winds, good radiational\ncooling will result in the coldest night of the fall so far.\nMost areas will fall into the mid to upper 20s, with some lows\nin the low 30s in the Hudson Valley where winds will take the\nlongest to decrease. Have adjusted temps up a few degrees from\nwhat was forecasted earlier today based on the latest NBM and\n3km HRRR, but this was only a subtle change by a change or two.\nDespite this change, most areas should still see sub-freezing \ntemps tonight, although the growing season has been already \ndeclared over due to being past the median first freeze date.\n\nTemps will continue to below normal for Sunday, although it\nwon't be as cool as Saturday, with highs mainly in the low to\nmid 50s (some upper 40s over the Adirondacks). High pressure\nmoving closer to the area will keep winds light and skies fairly\nclear. Temps will be chilly again on Sunday night, with lows in\nthe low to mid 30s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nA Nor'Easter is expected to develop off the coast of the mid\nAtlantic for this weekend and continue during the upcoming \nweek. This will be a slow moving storm system that will be\ncutoff to the south of the region. Although there remains some\nuncertainty within the guidance regarding the exact track and\nlocation of the surface low (especially within the AI models \nvs. the physics-based guidance), most guidance suggests that \nany impacts should remain south of the region, with just a few\nensemble members suggesting a few light showers for southeastern\nareas. As a result, the forecast is for dry weather through the\nupcoming week. With ridging overhead and surface high pressure\nnearby, there should be a decent amount of sunshine each day\nwith fairly light winds, although southeastern areas could see a\nbreeze towards mid-week if the Nor'Easter is closer to the\ncoast. Temperatures will be near seasonal normals each day, with\nhighs generally in the upper 50s to low 60s for valley areas and \n30s and 40s at night.",
